---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'FR Shirt Compliance for Vegas Electricians: NFPA 70E Without the Headache',
 description:
 'A practical overview of arc-rated apparel for Las Vegas electrical crews, including hazard assessment, garment selection, decoration, care, and inspection.',
 category: 'Compliance',
 readTime: '11 min read',
 date: 'Apr 7, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-04-07',
 image: '/images/generated/kit-electrical.jpg',
 slug: 'nfpa-70e-fr-shirts-vegas-electricians',
}

const faqs = [
 { q: 'Do Las Vegas electricians need CAT 1 or CAT 2 arc-rated shirts?', a: 'There is no universal category for a job title, voltage, or location. The employer must select PPE from its documented arc-flash risk assessment and current electrical-safety program. Use the incident-energy analysis or the PPE-category method as applicable, rather than treating CAT 2 as a default.' },
 { q: 'Can you embroider a company logo on arc-rated garments?', a: 'Decoration must follow the exact garment manufacturer\'s current instructions and the employer\'s safety requirements. Thread, backing, placement, size, and the complete decoration system can matter. Obtain written approval or guidance from the garment manufacturer and use a decorator qualified for protective apparel.' },
 { q: 'Which arc-rated shirt works best in Las Vegas heat?', a: 'Heat comfort matters because PPE must remain wearable, but it cannot override the required protection. Compare currently rated garments only after the employer establishes the hazard and required arc rating, then evaluate fit, weight, ventilation, care instructions, and compatibility with the complete PPE system.' },
 { q: 'Does electrical PPE require arc-rated pants as well as a shirt?', a: 'The required clothing system depends on the hazard assessment and must protect all exposed areas specified by the employer\'s electrical-safety program. Do not assume that one rated shirt makes an otherwise unmatched outfit compliant.' },
 { q: 'How often should arc-rated work shirts be replaced?', a: 'There is no single wash-count replacement rule for every garment. Follow the manufacturer\'s care, inspection, contamination, repair, and retirement instructions. Remove a garment from service when its label or manufacturer guidance says its protective performance may be compromised.' },
 { q: 'What determines the required arc-rated apparel?', a: 'The employer\'s electrical-safety program and task-specific arc-flash risk assessment determine whether arc-rated clothing is required and what rating the complete PPE system needs. A garment seller cannot determine that requirement from a job title or voltage alone.' },
]

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
 {
 '@type': 'Article',
 headline: post.title,
 description: post.description,
 image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`,
 datePublished: post.isoDate,
 dateModified: '2026-08-21',
 author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
 publisher: {
 '@type': 'Organization',
 name: 'Bighorn Threads',
 logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png' },
 },
 mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 },
 {
 '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
 itemListElement: [
 { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
 { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/blog/' },
 { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: post.title, item: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/` },
 ],
 },
 { '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })) },
 ],
}

const speakableSchema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@type': 'WebPage',
 url: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 name: post.title,
 speakable: {
  '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification',
  cssSelector: ['h1', '.speakable-answer'],
 },
}
---

<BaseLayout
 title={post.title}
 description={post.description}
 canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`}
 ogImage={post.image}
 type="article"
 publishedDate={post.isoDate}
>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(speakableSchema)} />

 <article class="bg-concrete">
 <header class="border-b border-hairline bg-paper">
 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:px-6 sm:pt-20 lg:px-8">
 <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-ash" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
 <a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-600">Home</a>
 <span class="px-2">/</span>
 <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gold-600">Blog</a>
 </nav>

 <span class="kicker mb-3">{post.category}</span>
 <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-extrabold uppercase leading-tight tracking-tight text-charcoal sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">{post.title}</h1>
 <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">
 How hazard assessment drives apparel selection, what to consider in Vegas heat, and how to verify decoration, care, and inspection requirements.
 </p>

 <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash">
 <span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span>
 <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
 <span>{post.readTime}</span>
 <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
 <time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time>
 </div>
 </div>
 </header>

 <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
 <img src={post.image} alt="Representative electrical-work apparel shown for employer PPE planning" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" class="w-full border border-hairline object-cover" />
 </div>

 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-gold-500/5 p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">There is no universal CAT rating for Las Vegas electricians. The employer's task-specific arc-flash risk assessment and electrical-safety program determine the required arc rating and complete PPE system. Any decoration must follow the exact garment manufacturer's current written instructions.</p>
 </aside>
 <aside class="mb-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6 lg:sticky lg:top-24">
 <h2 class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">In this article</h2>
 <ol class="space-y-2 font-mono text-sm text-ash">
 <li><a href="#what-is-nfpa-70e" class="hover:text-gold-600">1. What NFPA 70E actually covers</a></li>
 <li><a href="#cat-ratings" class="hover:text-gold-600">2. CAT 1 vs CAT 2 vs CAT 4</a></li>
 <li><a href="#brands" class="hover:text-gold-600">3. Comparing current garment options</a></li>
 <li><a href="#decoration" class="hover:text-gold-600">4. Reviewing decoration requirements</a></li>
 <li><a href="#mistakes" class="hover:text-gold-600">5. Five risks to review</a></li>
 <li><a href="#what-to-buy" class="hover:text-gold-600">6. What to actually buy</a></li>
 </ol>
 </aside>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Protective apparel decisions can fail when the garment is selected before the hazard is assessed, decoration requirements are not verified, or heat and fit make the chosen system impractical to wear. Treat apparel as one part of the employer's electrical-safety program, not as a standalone compliance checkbox.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
This article is not a substitute for NFPA 70E, OSHA requirements, a qualified safety professional, or the garment manufacturer's instructions. Bighorn Threads coordinates approved decoration through production partners. The employer and its safety team remain responsible for hazard assessment and PPE selection.
 </p>

 <h2 id="what-is-nfpa-70e" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">1. What NFPA 70E Actually Covers</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 NFPA 70E addresses electrical safety in the workplace, including work practices, risk assessment, and PPE for electrical hazards. Employers should use the current edition alongside applicable OSHA requirements and their own documented electrical-safety program.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 The required PPE cannot be inferred from a trade, equipment name, or voltage alone. A qualified person must evaluate the specific task and equipment conditions using the method adopted by the employer's safety program.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Arc-rated and flame-resistant are not interchangeable specifications. Review the exact product label and current technical data. The employer must select the complete system required by its task-specific assessment.
 </p>

 <h2 id="cat-ratings" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">2. CAT 1 vs CAT 2 vs CAT 4 — What Your Crew Actually Needs</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 NFPA 70E provides methods for selecting arc-flash PPE. Employers must apply the appropriate method consistently and select a complete system whose arc rating meets the assessed exposure. The category summaries below are reference points, not job assignments.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">CAT 1 (4 cal/cm² minimum)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Category 1 corresponds to a minimum arc rating of 4 cal/cm² when the PPE-category method applies. The complete clothing and PPE requirements must come from the current standard and the employer's assessment.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">CAT 2 (8 cal/cm² minimum)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Category 2 corresponds to a minimum arc rating of 8 cal/cm² when the PPE-category method applies. Do not assume that fabric weight, a particular layer combination, or commercial work automatically meets this requirement; verify the rating of the complete system.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">CAT 3 and CAT 4 (25 cal/cm² and 40 cal/cm²)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Categories 3 and 4 correspond to higher minimum arc ratings when the PPE-category method applies. These systems require specialized selection by the employer's qualified safety team.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border-l-4 border-gold-500 bg-panel p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Practical answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Start with the task-specific assessment, not a default shirt category. Once the required protection is documented, compare complete rated systems for heat burden, fit, mobility, care, availability, and compatibility with the rest of the PPE.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="brands" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">3. Comparing Current Garment Options</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Heat burden, fit, mobility, care, and availability matter after the required protection is established. Compare only currently rated garments that fit the employer's complete PPE system.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Bulwark FR</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Bulwark offers multiple arc-rated garments used in electrical work. Confirm the exact model's current label, arc rating, care instructions, and compatibility with the employer's required PPE system rather than relying on a brand or fabric weight alone.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Carhartt FR</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Carhartt offers arc-rated workwear in several fits and fabrics. Verify the current product label and technical data for the exact SKU, then evaluate comfort and fit with the complete required PPE. See our <a href="/brands/carhartt-custom-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">custom Carhartt page</a> for available options.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Wrangler Riggs FR</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Wrangler offers arc-rated workwear with traditional work-shirt styling. Confirm the rating and instructions for the exact garment before including it in a PPE program.
 </p>

 <h2 id="decoration" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">4. Embroidery Without Voiding the Rating</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Decoration can affect protective apparel and must be evaluated as a complete system. Follow the exact garment manufacturer's current written instructions for thread, backing, placement, size, patches, inks, and any other added material. When the instructions are unclear, obtain written guidance before decorating.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">FR-compatible thread</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Some manufacturers or safety programs specify aramid or other compatible materials for particular garments. Thread alone does not establish that a decorated garment remains compliant. Bighorn asks the selected production partner to follow the authorized specification for the exact garment and application.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Placement rules</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Placement and size limits vary by manufacturer, garment, and decoration method. Bighorn coordinates a review of the current instructions with the selected partner. The employer must approve the proposed decoration before production.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Screen printing on FR</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Screen printing may be permitted for some protective garments and prohibited or restricted for others. Ink chemistry, coverage, placement, and curing all require manufacturer review. Do not assume embroidery and printing provide equivalent compliance.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border-l-4 border-gold-500 bg-panel p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Why this matters</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Unsupported decoration can create an avoidable gap in a PPE program. Keep the garment manufacturer's written decoration guidance and the decorator's production specifications with the employer's PPE records.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="mistakes" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">5. Five Risks to Review</h2>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">1. Buying FR shirts but skipping FR pants</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A shirt's rating does not establish the protection of the complete outfit. Select every clothing layer and PPE component according to the hazard assessment, current standard, and employer's safety program.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">2. Layering a non-FR cotton tee under an FR shirt</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Do not improvise underlayers. Follow the current standard, employer's program, and garment-system instructions for permissible materials and layering; the rating of one outer garment does not automatically rate the combination.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">3. Treating the rating as permanent</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Protective garments require regular inspection and must be cared for and retired according to the exact manufacturer's instructions. There is no universal replacement interval or wash count. Remove contaminated, damaged, altered, or illegibly labeled garments from service when required by the manufacturer or employer's program.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">4. Using consumer detergent</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Use only the laundering products, temperatures, processes, and repair methods permitted on the garment's care label and in the manufacturer's current instructions. If a commercial laundry is used, verify that its process is appropriate for the exact protective garments.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">5. Embroidering with standard polyester thread</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Ask the decorator to identify the complete decoration specification, not just the thread. The shop should be able to show how its materials, placement, backing, and process follow the exact garment manufacturer's written guidance.
 </p>

 <h2 id="what-to-buy" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">6. What to Actually Buy</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 After the employer documents the required PPE system, the following inventory questions can help with ordering. These quantities and categories are operational examples, not a safety specification:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Enough approved shirts and pants</strong> for the employer's work and laundering schedule</li>
 <li><strong>Approved layers</strong> for the expected weather and required PPE system</li>
 <li><strong>Visibility garments</strong> selected for the job's applicable requirements and compatibility with arc-flash PPE</li>
 <li><strong>Documented decoration</strong> that follows the exact garment manufacturer's current instructions</li>
 <li><strong>Inspection and replacement inventory</strong> so damaged or contaminated garments can be removed promptly</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 For more planning guidance, see our <a href="/industries/electrical-contractors-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">electrical contractors page</a>. A proposed <a href="/company-stores/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">company store</a> may present approved products and ordering rules. Availability, approvals, payment, delivery, and fulfillment depend on the written setup.
 </p>

 <div class="my-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-8 text-center">
 <h3 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-extrabold uppercase tracking-tight text-charcoal">Want a quote on FR for your crew?</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-base text-ash">Send the employer-approved specification, roster, product requirements, and deadline for a current quote.</p>
 <a href="/get-a-quote/" class="btn-primary text-base">Get a Quote</a>
 </div>

 <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">The Short Version</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Start with the employer's task-specific hazard assessment. Select a complete PPE system that meets it, verify the exact garment and decoration instructions, and follow the manufacturer's care, inspection, and retirement criteria.
 </p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Apparel is only one part of electrical safety. Qualified work practices, equipment condition, boundaries, training, and the employer's full safety program remain essential.
 </p>

 <div class="mt-16 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6">
 <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
 <div class="flex h-14 w-14 shrink-0 items-center justify-center bg-gold-500/15">
 <span class="font-mono text-lg font-bold text-gold-700">BT</span>
 </div>
